.Dt XTC_LOG 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_log_create ,
.Nm xtc_log_destroy ,
.Nm xtc_log_write ,
.Nm xtc_log_vwrite ,
.Nm xtc_log_drain ,
.Nm xtc_log_set_floor ,
.Nm xtc_log_set_default ,
.Nm xtc_log_swap_default ,
.Nm xtc_log_default ,
.Nm xtc_log_drop_count ,
.Nm xtc_logf ,
.Nm xtc_logf_to ,
.Nm xtc_logf_impl ,
.Nm xtc_logarg ,
.Nm XTC_LOG_DEBUG_F ,
.Nm XTC_LOG_INFO_F ,
.Nm XTC_LOG_WARN_F ,
.Nm XTC_LOG_ERROR_F
.Nd async ring-buffer logger
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_log.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_create "const xtc_log_opts_t *opts" "xtc_log_t **out"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_log_destroy "xtc_log_t *log"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_write "xtc_log_t *log" "xtc_log_level_t level" "const char *fmt" "..."
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_log_vwrite "xtc_log_t *log" "xtc_log_level_t lvl" "const char *fmt" "va_list ap"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_drain "xtc_log_t *log"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_set_floor "xtc_log_t *log" "xtc_log_level_t lvl"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_set_default "xtc_log_t *log"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_swap_default "xtc_log_t *log" "xtc_log_t **out_prev"
.Ft "xtc_log_t *"
.Fn xtc_log_default "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_log_drop_count "const xtc_log_t *log"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_logf "xtc_log_level_t lvl" "const char *fmt" "..."
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_logf_to "xtc_log_t *log" "xtc_log_level_t lvl" "const char *fmt" "..."
.Ft void
.Fo xtc_logf_impl
.Fa "xtc_log_t *log"
.Fa "xtc_log_level_t lvl"
.Fa "const char *fmt"
.Fa "int nargs"
.Fa "const xtc_logarg_t *args"
.Fc
.Ft xtc_logarg_t
.Fn xtc_logarg "value"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_log
is a predictable-latency logger: callers append records to an
in-memory ring buffer; a sink consumer drains the ring on a timer
or explicit flush.  Producers never block.
Writers may run on any thread, concurrently with one another and with
the drain.
A record reaches the sink whole or not at all, and each writer's
records reach it in the order it wrote them; the drain stops at a
record a writer is still filling and the next drain resumes there.
When the ring is full the writer drops the oldest record to make
room, or, if that record is at that moment being written or read,
drops its own; either way
.Fn xtc_log_drop_count
counts it, so records printed plus records dropped equals records
written.
.Pp
Levels:
.Dv XTC_LOG_DEBUG ,
.Dv XTC_LOG_INFO ,
.Dv XTC_LOG_WARN ,
.Dv XTC_LOG_ERROR .
A configurable global level filter elides cheaper records before
formatting.
.Pp
.Sy Sinks
.Pq Va opts.sink_fd
or
.Va opts.sink_fn :
records are written to either a file descriptor (stdout, syslog
fd, etc.) or a user callback.
.Pp
The
.Fn XTC_LOG_*_F
macros are convenience wrappers that look up a process-default
.Vt xtc_log_t
and call
.Fn xtc_log_write
with the right level.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_log_drain
forces an immediate flush; called from the metrics proc on a
timer or at process exit.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_log_vwrite
is the
.Vt va_list
variant of
.Fn xtc_log_write
for callers that already hold a
.Fa ap .
.Fn xtc_log_set_floor
sets a per-logger minimum level below which records are dropped
before formatting.
.Fn xtc_log_set_default
installs
.Fa log
(or
.Dv NULL )
as the process-wide default the
.Fn XTC_LOG_*_F
macros route through, and returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
libxtc's own diagnostics (the
.Xr xtc_stats 3
tuning advisor, a loop's over-budget warning, a slab redzone report,
an executor step error) go to the same default; there is no default
until one is set, and those messages are dropped until then.
.Fn xtc_log_swap_default
does the same and stores the logger it replaced in
.Fa *out_prev ,
in one atomic exchange.
A writer that loaded the old default just before the swap may still be
inside
.Fn xtc_log_write
on it, so do not destroy a logger right after swapping it out unless
no other thread can be logging.
.Fn xtc_log_default
returns the current default.
.Fn xtc_log_drop_count
reports how many records were dropped because the ring was full.
.Ss Typed records
.Fn xtc_logf
writes a record to the default logger, and
.Fn xtc_logf_to
to
.Fa log .
They are function-like macros.
Each
.Ql {}
in
.Fa fmt
takes the next argument and formats it by that argument's own type, so a
call has no conversion letter to get wrong and no
.Dv PRIu64 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_logf(XTC_LOG_INFO, "loop {} drained {} bytes in {} us",
    loop_id, nbytes, us);
.Ed
.Pp
An argument of any integer type prints as its value, whatever its width
or signedness:
.Vt int8_t
to
.Vt uint64_t ,
.Vt size_t ,
.Vt ssize_t ,
.Vt ptrdiff_t ,
.Vt intptr_t ,
.Vt off_t ,
.Vt pid_t ,
an enumeration,
.Vt char ,
and
.Vt _Bool
(0 or 1).
A character literal is an
.Vt int
in C, so
.Ql 'x'
prints 120.
A
.Vt float
or
.Vt double
prints as with
.Ql %g .
A
.Vt "char *"
or
.Vt "const char *"
prints as the string, and a null one as
.Ql (null) .
Any other object pointer prints as
.Ql 0x
and its address in hex.
With
.In xtc_proc.h ,
an
.Vt xtc_pid_t
prints as
.Ql <loop_id.local_id.gen> .
A struct or union argument does not compile; nor does a function pointer
in C++ (C converts one to a data pointer, which ISO C leaves undefined,
and
.Fl Wpedantic
warns).
gcc gives a bit-field a type of its own, which gcc 14 rejects: cast it.
.Pp
A spec after a colon changes the format:
.Bl -tag -width "{:08x}" -offset indent
.It Li {:x} {:X}
hex, lower or upper case
.It Li {:o}
octal
.It Li {:8}
at least 8 characters, padded on the left with spaces
.It Li {:08x}
zero-filled to 8 characters (for a number)
.It Li {:.3}
3 decimal places for a floating value, at most 3 characters of a string
.It Li {:p}
an address in hex, a string's included
.El
.Pp
A negative integer in hex or octal prints as its two's complement in its
own width: an
.Vt int8_t
of \-1 is
.Ql ff .
A spec letter that does not apply to the argument's type is ignored.
.Ql {{
and
.Ql }}
print one brace.
A brace that does not start a well-formed placeholder prints as itself
and is not counted.
.Pp
A call takes at most 16 arguments after
.Fa fmt ;
a 17th is a compile error.
When the placeholders and the arguments do not match, the record is still
written, starting with a marker such as
.Ql "[xtc_logf: 2 args, 3 {}] " ;
a placeholder with no argument prints as written, and an argument with no
placeholder is not printed.
The marker comes first so that truncation to
.Va record_max
cannot cut it off.
A mismatch is never undefined behaviour.
.Pp
The record path is the one
.Fn xtc_log_write
takes: the level floor (checked before any formatting), the ring, the
sink, the 1024-byte line and
.Va record_max
truncation, and
.Fn xtc_log_drop_count .
As with any function call, each argument is evaluated once, before the
level is checked.
.Pp
C needs C11
.Pq Li _Generic ;
Microsoft C needs
.Li /std:c11
(Visual Studio 2019 16.8 or later), which also selects the conforming
preprocessor the argument count relies on.
C++ needs C++11: the same calls resolve through overloads of
.Fn xtc_logarg ,
and a scoped enumeration needs a cast.
Under C++98 the header still compiles, without
.Fn xtc_logf .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_logarg
converts one value to an
.Vt xtc_logarg_t ,
and
.Fn xtc_logf_impl
formats
.Fa fmt
with the
.Fa nargs
converted arguments in
.Fa args
and appends the record; the macros expand to these two.
Calling them directly is supported, but
.Fn xtc_logf
is the interface.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_log_t *log;
xtc_log_opts_t opts = XTC_LOG_OPTS_DEFAULT;
opts.sink_fd = STDOUT_FILENO;
opts.floor = XTC_LOG_INFO;

xtc_log_create(&opts, &log);
xtc_log_set_default(log);
XTC_LOG_INFO_F("server started on port %d", port);
xtc_logf(XTC_LOG_INFO, "listening on {} with {} workers", port, nworkers);
xtc_log_drain(log);    /* from a thread that may block on the sink */
.Ed
